# Execute Domain Actions: Preview, Row Guards, and Bulk Rollback

An enabled action can become invalid before the user clicks Apply. This workbook
checks declarative guards in the actual PostgreSQL update and verifies the data
after both success and failure. It uses five synthetic rows in one temporary
table. Set `SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB*` for an existing disposable PostgreSQL database.
No Ecto Repo, migrations, or e-commerce seeds are needed.

## Setup

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_updato!()
```

## 1) Start the fixture and define the host execution boundary

The [fixture](support/action_fixture.exs) supplies the same domain used in the
[planning workbook](selecto_actions_workbook.livemd). The host adapter owns the
connection and tenant context. Preserve the context provided by
`ActionExecutionAdapter`: it includes phase-bound authorization evidence.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/action_fixture.exs", __DIR__))
connection = SelectoLivebooks.ActionFixture.connect!()
domain = SelectoLivebooks.ActionFixture.domain()
runtime = Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, connection)
target = Selecto.configure(domain, runtime)

defmodule ApprovalHost do
  @behaviour SelectoUpdato.ActionExecutionAdapter

  def execute_action(plan, context, _opts) do
    operation = SelectoUpdato.with_tenant(plan.operation_builder, context.tenant)
    SelectoUpdato.execute(operation, context.query_target, context: context)
  end

  def dry_run_action(plan, context, _opts) do
    operation = SelectoUpdato.with_tenant(plan.operation_builder, context.tenant)
    SelectoUpdato.preview(operation, context.query_target, context: context)
  end
end

context = %{actor: %{id: 17, role: :approver}, tenant: %{tenant_id: 101}, query_target: target}
policy = fn request, ctx, _opts ->
  if request.capability == "approvals.approve" and ctx.actor.role == :approver,
    do: :enabled, else: :hidden
end
plan_for = fn selected_target ->
  {:ok, plan} = SelectoUpdato.plan_domain_action(domain,
    %{action: :approve, target: selected_target}, trusted_context: %{actor_id: context.actor.id})
  plan
end
snapshot = fn ->
  Postgrex.query!(connection,
    "SELECT id, tenant_id, state, ready, reviewed_by_id FROM livebook_approvals ORDER BY id", []).rows
end
initial_rows = snapshot.()
```

## 2) Preview without changing a row

Preview compiles SQL under a preview authorization grant. The transition and
readiness guard are in the same update predicate as the target and tenant.
The host can show the statement and bound values to an operator.

```elixir
plan = plan_for.(1)
assert {:ok, preview} = SelectoUpdato.ActionExecutionAdapter.dry_run(
  ApprovalHost, plan, context, capability_resolver: policy)
assert snapshot.() == initial_rows
assert [%{text: sql, params: params}] = preview.statements
assert sql =~ ~r/\bupdate\b/i
for field <- ["id", "tenant_id", "state", "ready"] do
  assert sql =~ field
end
assert 101 in params
assert "open" in params
assert true in params
%{sql: sql, params: params}
```

## 3) Missing or denied authorization leaves data unchanged

```elixir
assert {:error, {:validation_error, _, %{code: :missing_capability_resolver}}} =
  SelectoUpdato.ActionExecutionAdapter.execute(ApprovalHost, plan, context)
denied_context = put_in(context, [:actor, :role], :reader)
assert {:error, {:validation_error, _, %{status: :hidden}}} =
  SelectoUpdato.ActionExecutionAdapter.execute(ApprovalHost, plan, denied_context,
    capability_resolver: policy)
assert snapshot.() == initial_rows
```

## 4) Execute, then reject a repeated transition

```elixir
assert {:ok, result} = SelectoUpdato.ActionExecutionAdapter.execute(
  ApprovalHost, plan, context, capability_resolver: policy)
assert result.affected_rows == 1
after_approval = snapshot.()
assert [1, 101, "approved", true, 17] in after_approval

assert {:error, %SelectoUpdato.Error{type: :cardinality_mismatch,
  details: %{expected: {:exactly, 1}, actual: 0}}} = SelectoUpdato.ActionExecutionAdapter.execute(
  ApprovalHost, plan, context, capability_resolver: policy)
assert snapshot.() == after_approval
```

## 5) A plan cannot freeze eligibility or switch tenants

First plan for ready row 4, then simulate another request revoking readiness.
The update must reevaluate the guard. Row 3 belongs to tenant 202; a target ID
alone cannot bring it into tenant 101's write scope. SQL NULL also fails the
`ready = true` predicate for row 5.

```elixir
stale_plan = plan_for.(4)
Postgrex.query!(connection, "UPDATE livebook_approvals SET ready = false WHERE id = 4", [])
before_rejections = snapshot.()
for rejected_plan <- [stale_plan, plan_for.(3), plan_for.(5)] do
  assert {:error, %SelectoUpdato.Error{type: :cardinality_mismatch,
    details: %{expected: {:exactly, 1}, actual: 0}}} = SelectoUpdato.ActionExecutionAdapter.execute(
    ApprovalHost, rejected_plan, context, capability_resolver: policy)
  assert snapshot.() == before_rejections
end
```

## 6) Roll back a partially matching bulk selection

Restore row 4 for this experiment. Row 2 remains ineligible. The selected IDs
require two updates, but only one matches. The adapter enforces cardinality
inside a transaction, so row 4 must remain open after the failed bulk action.

```elixir
Postgrex.query!(connection, "UPDATE livebook_approvals SET ready = true WHERE id = 4", [])
before_bulk = snapshot.()
bulk_plan = plan_for.(%{ids: [2, 4]})
assert bulk_plan.expected_cardinality == {:exactly, 2}
assert {:error, %SelectoUpdato.Error{type: :cardinality_mismatch,
  details: %{expected: {:exactly, 2}, actual: 1}}} = SelectoUpdato.ActionExecutionAdapter.execute(
  ApprovalHost, bulk_plan, context, capability_resolver: policy)
assert snapshot.() == before_bulk
assert [4, 101, "open", true, nil] in before_bulk
```

The error details explain the failed match/cardinality contract. The assertions
also verify database state, so an error after a partial commit cannot pass.
UI eligibility and preview results help the user; execution-time authorization,
tenant scope, and row guards determine whether the mutation succeeds.

## 7) Close the fixture

```elixir
:ok = GenServer.stop(connection)
```

Closing the session removes its temporary table. These cases exercise the pinned
PostgreSQL adapter. A production host still owns confirmation handling, audit
persistence, authentication, and any operation-specific membership checks.
